---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PanelHead from '../../components/PanelHead.astro';

// THE CASE STUDY, moved in with the other eight (chooser: metrotuner-two-pages,
// Q1/02 "instrument first"). /metrotuner/ is the instrument now; this page is the
// writeup, and the one place the two meet is the link under the dek and the "about"
// word at the instrument's foot.
//
// TYPE B (skill shape): dek → a named head over the argument → the facts colophon.
// The href passed to PanelHead is the ENTRY's href in experiments.ts, which points
// HERE now — the index row opens the writeup, and the instrument is one click
// further ("open the instrument" under the dek).
---

<Layout
  title="Metrotuner — Andrew Shiau"
  description="How the browser tuner and metronome were built: McLeod pitch detection, a lookahead scheduler, and a test suite with a fake microphone."
>
  <div class="grid">
    <PanelHead href="/work/metrotuner/" title="Metrotuner">
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Pitch</div>
        <div class="ftx">McLeod pitch method, asserted to half a cent in the unit suite</div>
      </div>
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Microphone</div>
        <div class="ftx">Read in the page and discarded. Nothing is recorded or sent.</div>
      </div>
    </PanelHead>

    <div class="feed">
      <div class="grid">
        <p class="dek c1-12">
          A chromatic tuner and a metronome, side by side on one screen. Pitch
          detection runs in the browser; the microphone signal never leaves it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid">
        <div class="c1-12">
          <p><a href="/metrotuner/">Open the instrument</a> — the tuner reads the
          offset in cents, the metronome takes a typed tempo or a tapped one, and on
          a phone you swipe between them.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE B beat 2: a named head over the argument. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>The detector that failed first</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              The obvious pitch detector is autocorrelation, and it failed its own
              tests. The raw correlation tapers with lag, which dragged a <b>65 Hz</b>
              sine <b>22 cents</b> sharp; normalizing the taper out made every multiple
              of the true period score the same, and the picker grabbed nine times the
              period — a reading three octaves flat.
            </p>
            <p>
              What shipped is the <b>McLeod pitch method</b>: a normalized square
              difference bounded to ±1 at every lag, with the first key maximum within
              10% of the best taken as the period. On synthesized buffers it reads
              within <b>0.25 cents</b> from C2 to C8, including a plucked-string case
              where the second harmonic carries more energy than the fundamental — the
              case that makes FFT-peak tuners read a low E as an octave up.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>Testing an instrument without an instrument</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              The unit suite runs the math on synthesized buffers: <b>39</b> assertions
              covering the note grid, the scheduler, tap tempo, and the detector's
              accuracy bounds. Direction is asserted, not just magnitude — a tuner with
              a flipped sign passes every "about 23 cents" check and tunes every string
              the wrong way.
            </p>
            <p>
              The end-to-end suite plays known sine waves into Chromium's fake
              microphone: <b>440 Hz</b> must read A4 at 0 cents, <b>446 Hz</b> must
              read sharp with a plus sign, and recalibrating A4 to 446 must move the
              same input to 0 — the control that proves the calibration is wired
              through rather than merely stored. The metronome's ticks are asserted on
              the audio clock, where the scheduler computes times instead of
              accumulating them.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>One screen, two instruments</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              The instrument page escapes the site's document measure — the one page
              that does. On a wide screen the tuner and metronome centre in equal
              halves, both running. Under 900px the halves become two swipe pages, and
              the indicator is the site's own pair: a disc for the page on screen, a
              ring for the other. Every treatment was picked from a proof sheet where
              the options ran live off one simulated signal.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE B close: the facts colophon, read after the work. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="c1-12 colo">
          <div><span class="ck">Pitch detection</span>McLeod pitch method on a 2048-sample window — normalized square difference, key-maxima picking, parabolic interpolation.</div>
          <div><span class="ck">Timing</span>A lookahead scheduler on the audio clock. setInterval wakes it; the audio thread keeps time.</div>
          <div><span class="ck">Sounds</span>Three synthesized sine pings — G6 for the downbeat, C6 for the beat, G5 under it. No samples.</div>
          <div><span class="ck">Privacy</span>The microphone signal is analysed in the page and discarded. Nothing is recorded, stored, or sent.</div>
          <div><span class="ck">Apparatus</span>Web Audio API and 0 dependencies. 39 unit tests on the math; 14 end-to-end tests against a fake microphone, re-run against the live site after every deploy.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .colo > div + div{margin-top:var(--lead)}
  .colo > div{max-width:62ch}
</style>
